6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Col6's boundary-equality reading is the cleanest way to reconcile the quota formulas with the given ballots. The original six voters start as a 3-cohesive group on {a,b,c} whose union is exactly {a,b,c,x,y,z}. For any size-100 committee that leaves voter 1 unsatisfied, at most y and z from that union can enter W. The PJR condition |N'| ≥ ℓ n/100 therefore ceases to require ℓ=3 precisely when equality holds at n=200 (6 = 3·200/100). At that point y,z plus 98 dummies already meet every lower-cohesiveness subgroup, and 194 fresh-candidate dummies introduce no new binding groups.  

EJR inherits the same equality threshold but tightens the per-voter demand because y and z are approved by disjoint subsets; the ℓ=1 quota therefore drops to equality at n=300. The identical dummy construction works, so the minimal extensions are exactly s₁=200 and s₂=300.  

The strict-inequality arguments that produce 201/301 simply add an extra voter that the definitions do not require; the 601 figures over-apply the ℓ=1 test to the whole electorate rather than to the original six. Checking every proper subgroup of the original group confirms that the whole group supplies the binding equality point, leaving no smaller extension admissible.}

